1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,003
Every PM has written a PRD that sailed through review: clear problem statement,

2
00:00:05,003 --> 00:00:08,243
clean user stories, a tidy list of acceptance criteria.

3
00:00:08,243 --> 00:00:09,480
Engineering built it.

4
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:10,716
QA checked the boxes.

5
00:00:10,716 --> 00:00:11,364
It shipped.

6
00:00:11,364 --> 00:00:16,253
And then it was wrong, in production, in a way nobody in that review had a sentence

7
00:00:16,253 --> 00:00:16,960
to point to.

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:18,315
So what actually broke?

9
00:00:18,315 --> 00:00:19,257
Not the writing.

10
00:00:19,257 --> 00:00:24,146
A normal PRD makes one assumption so basic nobody states it, and that assumption is

11
00:00:24,146 --> 00:00:26,325
exactly the one an AI feature breaks.

12
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:30,507
The postmortem for that kind of launch almost never blames the writing.

13
00:00:30,507 --> 00:00:35,160
It blames "we should have tested more" or "the model wasn't ready", and both of

14
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:37,163
those miss what actually happened.

15
00:00:37,163 --> 00:00:42,051
The document itself had no place to write down what "ready" would have looked like.

16
00:00:42,051 --> 00:00:46,705
You cannot test your way out of a specification that never defined what passing

17
00:00:46,705 --> 00:00:47,058
means.

18
00:00:47,608 --> 00:00:52,186
Remember the very first idea this course gave you: an AI feature is a distribution,

19
00:00:52,186 --> 00:00:53,013
not a function.

20
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:57,591
Here's where that stops being a slogan and starts being the reason your PRD doesn't

21
00:00:57,591 --> 00:00:57,867
work.

22
00:00:57,867 --> 00:01:02,225
A normal acceptance criterion assumes you can run the feature once and get your

23
00:01:02,225 --> 00:01:03,824
answer: does it pass, or not.

24
00:01:03,824 --> 00:01:08,457
Run an AI feature once and you've learned almost nothing, because the next run, same

25
00:01:08,457 --> 00:01:10,167
input, can come back different.

26
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:13,201
A single wrong answer isn't a bug to reproduce and fix.

27
00:01:13,201 --> 00:01:16,455
It's one draw from a distribution you haven't measured yet.

28
00:01:16,455 --> 00:01:18,551
That changes what "works" has to mean.

29
00:01:18,551 --> 00:01:22,908
Not a checklist item, a threshold: clears some bar, on some sample, some agreed

30
00:01:22,908 --> 00:01:24,177
percentage of the time.

31
00:01:24,177 --> 00:01:25,942
And QA can't verify that by eye.

32
00:01:25,942 --> 00:01:30,299
They need the rubric this section is about to give you, not a gut feeling about

33
00:01:30,299 --> 00:01:31,954
whether an answer looks right.

34
00:01:32,504 --> 00:01:35,894
It's the row that trips up experienced PMs, not just new ones.

35
00:01:35,894 --> 00:01:40,377
The real question isn't "did we fix it", it's "did we get the rate low enough, and

36
00:01:40,377 --> 00:01:44,641
do we know what that rate actually is." That's a different kind of engineering

37
00:01:44,641 --> 00:01:47,757
conversation, and it starts with a different kind of PRD.

38
00:01:48,307 --> 00:01:52,762
Here's a sentence that passed a real review, and it's worth taking apart, because

39
00:01:52,762 --> 00:01:56,392
every word in it feels reasonable and not one of them is testable.

40
00:01:56,392 --> 00:01:57,987
Helpfully, by whose judgment.

41
00:01:57,987 --> 00:01:59,801
Accurately, checked against what.

42
00:01:59,801 --> 00:02:04,146
Customer questions, all of them, or the ones that happen to come up in testing.

43
00:02:04,146 --> 00:02:08,766
There is no version of this sentence a QA engineer can run against a feature and get

44
00:02:08,766 --> 00:02:09,976
a pass or fail out of.

45
00:02:09,976 --> 00:02:11,240
It isn't wrong exactly.

46
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:13,110
It just never was a specification.

47
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:15,420
It was a hope, formatted to look like one.

48
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:19,655
Here's how you can tell the difference for real, on any line in front of you.

49
00:02:19,655 --> 00:02:23,944
Picture the worst plausible answer the feature could give, something genuinely

50
00:02:23,944 --> 00:02:27,904
wrong, and ask whether this sentence would have caught it before launch.

51
00:02:27,904 --> 00:02:32,194
"Helpful and accurate" doesn't catch anything, because whoever's defending the

52
00:02:32,194 --> 00:02:36,428
launch can just as easily insist the bad answer was helpful and accurate too.

53
00:02:36,428 --> 00:02:40,498
A real specification narrows that argument down to a fact, not an opinion.

54
00:02:41,048 --> 00:02:45,051
Say the honest caveat, because the instinct once you spot this is to add more

55
00:02:45,051 --> 00:02:47,235
adjectives, and that doesn't fix anything.

56
00:02:47,235 --> 00:02:50,042
More detail in the same shape is still the same shape.

57
00:02:50,042 --> 00:02:54,306
What's actually missing isn't better prose, it's a different structure entirely: a

58
00:02:54,306 --> 00:02:58,569
dataset to test against, a rubric that defines the bar, a threshold that counts as

59
00:02:58,569 --> 00:03:01,740
passing, and a plan for what happens on the cases that don't.

60
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:02,936
That's not a paragraph.

61
00:03:02,936 --> 00:03:04,600
That's the rest of this section.

62
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:09,675
That's the test worth applying to every acceptance criterion you've ever written for

63
00:03:09,675 --> 00:03:12,638
one of these features, not just the plainly vague ones.

64
00:03:12,638 --> 00:03:15,170
Do it for real, on a document you actually own.

65
00:03:15,170 --> 00:03:18,509
Find one line, and ask honestly whether it could fail a build.

66
00:03:18,509 --> 00:03:21,257
If you're not sure, that uncertainty is the answer.

67
00:03:21,807 --> 00:03:23,479
One thing before you move on.

68
00:03:23,479 --> 00:03:28,094
Find one acceptance criterion in a real PRD that couldn't actually fail a build.

69
00:03:28,094 --> 00:03:32,939
Then notice, specifically, what's missing: a sample to test against, a definition of

70
00:03:32,939 --> 00:03:35,881
good, a threshold, anything concrete enough to run.

71
00:03:35,881 --> 00:03:40,668
Next lecture turns that gap into the fix: acceptance criteria written as evaluation

72
00:03:40,668 --> 00:03:44,591
thresholds, the exact sentence that replaces "helpful and accurate."
